well the MP for Grantham in Stanford is  Nick Bowles who's been pushing a brexit  plan of his own I sat down with him and  started by asking him if he's going to  be backing the prime ministers deal it's  not a perfect deal I've had severe  reservations about it in the past  particularly the earlier version which  was going to carve Northern Ireland out  into a separate customs territory that's  not something that I think I would have  been able to vote for but actually I do  think that the latest version is is a  big improvement on that and of course  there are flaws of course there things  about it that I don't much like but I  certainly think that it is better than  no deal we do need to leave the EU in my  view so I will be voting for it and you  say you have some concerns about it I  mean those concerns are pretty widely  shared with lots of people in Parliament  on I mean the DP for example is saying  that they don't like the fact that  Northern Ireland will in their view be  treated differently to the rest of the  UK under the backstop others are unhappy  with the kind of close links to to the  EU or the fact that they think that the  future relationship is too flimsy a  release it's a real serious concerns on  them know there there are very serious  concerns and it is quite hard at this  point to see how the Prime Minister wins  because in the sense it's got into that  terrible position where lots of people  hate it for entirely different reasons  and that's not a very good place to be  if you're trying to win a majority in a  house where you don't have a permanent  majority oh it's gonna be difficult  isn't it I'm around a hundred  Conservative MPs who have expressed  opposition to the deal I mean there's no  way she's gonna get mr. e parliament is  that I think it's very unlikely that  she's going to win it's not completely  impossible I think that there has been  growing support I believe in the country  for the Prime Minister she's actually  been performing probably at her best in  the last few weeks and I think there's a  lot of public sympathy for the way that  she just keeps on going with all of  these people attacking her from all  sides same time that a few weeks not  very long I mean it's a bit too late  isn't it I think that's the problem in a  way I think it is probably too late to  turn it around if if in a sense the deal  had been announced a couple of months  ago  and she'd had the full two months to  talk to MPs individually to understand  their concerns to try and find ways to  address them maybe maybe maybe she could  have pulled it round but as it is I fear  she's not going to that she is going to  lose and then the question is what's  next well that is the question and you  have got a plan B haven't you just talk  you through what that is well I think  the most important thing about my plan B  is that it's nobody's plan a this is not  a perfect deal that I'm proposing but it  is the one that is doable what I'm  suggesting is that we should move into a  position a bit like norway's where we're  in the common market that binds Norway  Iceland and Liechtenstein with the EU  it's called the European Economic Area  and we join something called the  European free trade association and we  go into a temporary customs union with  EU until we've found new arrangements  that satisfy the border requirements to  the satisfaction of the Republic of  Ireland and the EU so we'd be in the  single market in a temporary customs  union but would be outside lots of  things that we don't like about the EU  would be a guide the common agricultural  and Fisheries policies would be outside  the European Court of Justice and would  be outside all of the political stuff of  the EU so it would be in a weird way  going back to the common market that we  joined you know decades ago that was  about trade and about economic  integration and we would no longer be  bound by this sort of drive towards ever  closer Union that the rest of the EU is  so committed to now it's one big problem  with your plan though isn't there and  that's immigration because if you do go  down the Norway route you have to accept  free movement of people I mean that  really is the Prime Minister's biggest  red line of all yeah the one big  downside there's no but avoiding it of  the Norway option is that freedom of  movement still applies now for many  people including many people in my  constituency and I'm very aware of that  the desire to control immigration was  one of the key motivations of their vote  to leave on the other hand the level of  Mayor migration from the EU is now at  its lowest level since 2012 just last  week we had the latest figures and we  also find in opinion polls that concern  about immigration has also subsided  quite fast but is that because they  think that free women it's going to end  well maybe tease or maybe it's because  they've seen that it is actually much  less of an issue than it was and there  is one thing about the European Economic  Area about the Norway position which is  that we would have this legal remedy  which is an emergency break which is  that if you have events that are causing  serious economic or societal  difficulties is the way it's put then  you can suspend one of the four freedoms  the freedom of movement for a period in  order to bring it under control now  nobody's used it and we don't exactly  know what it would deliver but it isn't  available to us as a member of the EU  and it would be available to us as a  member of the EEA and Theresa may have  said that this is not an option that  Norway's not going to happen  the Prime Minister is particularly  focused on immigration this has been  true of her in a sense  throughout her time in government as  Home Secretary and then as Prime  Minister and so it's no surprise I think  to anyone that she objects fundamentally  to the fact that the Norway option does  not control immigration in the way that  she'd like but I'm only proposing this  in the context where her deal has been  defeated and that's quite likely to  happen in ten days time now the question  is what else is there out there and the  real truth is this there is no other  option that the EU would support that  could win a majority in parliament and  get us out of the European Union on the  29th of March and for me that's the  overwhelming priority and are you  confident it could get through  Parliament than you having those  conversations yes over the last couple  of months I've been seeking out people  and all parties Labour Party the SNP  applied comer e the DUP Liberal  Democrats as well as Conservatives and  just in recent weeks people have been  seeking  and that ranges from you know many  members of the cabinet to leaders of  some of the other parties and it's  because they all see that at the moment  we might be heading off the cliff we're  gonna do a Thelma Louise and drive  straight off the cliff into the sunset  and that's not something that almost  anyone really wants to see and they do  see the potential of the Norway option  to get us out of the EU and avoid no  deal so how many members of the cabinet  you talking about it well I've spoken to  probably now six or eight members of the  cabinet I don't want to say that they're  all completely signed up to this as a  plan-b I mean of course they're all  supporting the Prime Minister's plan  first and foremost some of them are very  committed to it amber rod has been very  clear before she went back into the  cabinet that this was her preferred  fallback option others you know are  thinking it over but what is true of  everyone is that they're all looking for  that realistic doable negotiable  deliverable brexit deal and there aren't  any others if the Prime Minister's deal  is defeated and just take you step back  from the kind of intricacies of the  options on the table  you represent a seat that for many  people is almost synonymous with  conservatism it's where Margaret  Thatcher was born are you worried about  the Conservatives party image at the  minute with all of this rouse or the  brexit well there's no question the  Conservative Party is not a happy family  at the moment and we're not behaving in  a particularly adult or harmonious  fashion and and Margaret Thatcher would  be you know taking us all off no end I'm  sure for that the key thing is that we  need to be able to get beyond it and  soon we that's why I think a second  referendum would be so disastrous  because the whole country would carry on  arguing about this and in families and  in workplaces people would be falling  out with each other as we went through  the whole argument all over again do you  agree with your colleagues who say that  after brexit is they hope done next year  it's time for Theresa May to hand over  to a new generation of Ulster  I think we're definitely going to need  to have fresh faces new ideas and new  leadership before the next election I  think it should be up to tourism a to  decide precisely when she wants to hand  over my own view would be that it can't  be just before the next direction there  needs to be a decent period for someone  to be able to demonstrate what their new  approach and their new ideas would  deliver for the British people so I hope  that she would see mission completed on  the 29th of March as the time to start  thinking about when it is that she wants  to hand over to a successor
 

